WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:05.860
Everything we do today fits into three stages.

2
00:00:06.540 --> 00:00:07.720
Stage 1. Transport.

3
00:00:08.360 --> 00:00:13.120
ATP citrate lyase cleaves citrate to deliver acetyl CoA into the cytoplasm.

4
00:00:13.640 --> 00:00:15.400
Stage 2. Activation.

5
00:00:15.700 --> 00:00:16.700
The committed step.

6
00:00:16.860 --> 00:00:22.740
Acetyl CoA carboxylase spends an ATP to make malonyl CoA, locking the cell into

7
00:00:22.740 --> 00:00:24.580
building fat. Stage 3.

8
00:00:25.020 --> 00:00:30.700
Elongation. Fatty acid synthase runs a four-step cycle over and over, adding two

9
00:00:30.700 --> 00:00:33.160
carbons each pass until it reaches palmitate.

10
00:00:33.720 --> 00:00:38.300
Notice the logic: get the bricks to the site, commit irreversibly, then build.

11
00:00:39.040 --> 00:00:43.360
If you remember nothing else, remember transport, activation, elongation, in that

12
00:00:43.360 --> 00:00:44.360
order.

13
00:00:44.383 --> 00:00:46.883
Here is the decision point of the entire pathway.

14
00:00:47.623 --> 00:00:54.383
Acetyl CoA carboxylase takes acetyl CoA, bicarbonate, and ATP and produces malonyl

15
00:00:54.383 --> 00:00:59.923
CoA. This is the committed step, the reaction that says the cell is now making fat

16
00:00:59.923 --> 00:01:06.383
and nothing else. The enzyme depends on biotin, a vitamin cofactor whose job is to

17
00:01:06.383 --> 00:01:07.663
carry carbon dioxide.

18
00:01:08.443 --> 00:01:13.083
Think of biotin as a swinging arm that grabs a carboxyl group and delivers it.

19
00:01:13.463 --> 00:01:17.883
Because this step is committed and irreversible, it is exactly where regulation

20
00:01:17.883 --> 00:01:23.003
should sit. And indeed, this enzyme is the master control valve we will return to.

21
00:01:23.733 --> 00:01:29.593
Now we build. Fatty acid synthase is one enzyme complex that runs a repeating four

22
00:01:29.593 --> 00:01:34.273
-step cycle, condensation, reduction, dehydration, reduction.

23
00:01:35.013 --> 00:01:36.293
Remember it as CRDR.

24
00:01:37.213 --> 00:01:42.333
Each turn of the cycle joins the growing chain to a fresh two-carbon unit and then

25
00:01:42.333 --> 00:01:45.313
chemically grooms the new linkage into a saturated stretch.

26
00:01:45.993 --> 00:01:52.153
The cycle is not run once, but seven times, because each pass adds two carbons and

27
00:01:52.153 --> 00:01:55.493
we are aiming for a 16-carbon product, palmitate.

28
00:01:56.093 --> 00:02:00.693
The elegance is that a single enzyme assembly does the same four moves repeatedly,

29
00:02:01.233 --> 00:02:04.453
like a machine stamping out identical parts on an assembly line.

30
00:02:05.217 --> 00:02:07.857
Here is the chemical trick that makes synthesis go.

31
00:02:08.417 --> 00:02:12.437
We have a primer, the acetyl group, and an extender, the malonyl group.

32
00:02:12.877 --> 00:02:17.477
When they condense, the malonyl loses its extra carboxyl as carbon dioxide.

33
00:02:18.017 --> 00:02:20.337
That decarboxylation is the engine.

34
00:02:20.957 --> 00:02:25.577
Releasing carbon dioxide is so thermodynamically favorable that it drags the

35
00:02:25.577 --> 00:02:30.137
otherwise sluggish condensation forward, the way letting go of a heavy weight pulls

36
00:02:30.137 --> 00:02:31.217
the whole rope along.

37
00:02:31.537 --> 00:02:37.257
The product, acetoacetyl acp, forms readily because the cell paid for that carbon

38
00:02:37.257 --> 00:02:38.817
dioxide earlier with ATP.

39
00:02:39.377 --> 00:02:44.037
So the carbon we added in stage 2 leaves here, but its purpose was always to power

40
00:02:44.037 --> 00:02:45.037
this step.

41
00:02:45.400 --> 00:02:47.100
Step back and ask a fair question.

42
00:02:47.680 --> 00:02:53.400
Why spend an ATP to attach a carbon dioxide in stage two only to throw that same

43
00:02:53.400 --> 00:02:55.840
carbon dioxide away during condensation?

44
00:02:56.520 --> 00:02:57.520
It looks wasteful.

45
00:02:58.040 --> 00:03:00.800
The answer is that the cell is buying thermodynamics.

46
00:03:01.520 --> 00:03:06.260
Direct condensation of two acetyl units is unfavorable and would barely proceed.

47
00:03:06.780 --> 00:03:12.580
By investing one ATP to carboxylate first, the cell stores energy in malonyl CoA.

48
00:03:13.020 --> 00:03:17.840
Then, when that carbon dioxide is released, its departure makes condensation

49
00:03:17.840 --> 00:03:19.020
strongly downhill.

50
00:03:19.600 --> 00:03:25.100
It is a gambit. Pay up front, release on demand, and gain control over a reaction

51
00:03:25.100 --> 00:03:26.840
that otherwise would not run.

52
00:03:27.440 --> 00:03:30.220
Every carbon still traces back to acetyl CoA.
